<header><p class="st-kicker">other · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Carnatic Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1692 CE – 1855 CE · capital Arcot</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The state of the Nawabs of Arcot in south-east India, whose succession wars drew Britain and France into their struggle for India.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> conquered the Carnatic region after the fall of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/golconda-sultanate">Golconda Sultanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate</a>. In 1692 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulfiqar-khan">Zulfiqar Ali Khan</a> as Nawab of the Carnatic, and the governorship, based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcot">Arcot</a>, became hereditary under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saadatullah-khan-i">Saadatullah Khan</a>, nominally subordinate to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Nawabs ruled a largely <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> Tamil and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telugu-language">Telugu</a> population through local chiefs and revenue farmers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanda-sahib">Chanda Sahib</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> in 1736. Rival claims between Chanda Sahib, backed by the French, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-khan-wallajah">Muhammad Ali Khan Wallajah</a>, backed by the British, led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnatic-wars">Carnatic Wars</a> and the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcot">Arcot</a> in 1751, a turning point in British fortunes.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-khan-wallajah">Muhammad Ali Wallajah</a> won but fell deep into debt to British creditors. In 1801 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">British East India Company</a> took over the administration, leaving the nawabs a titular role, and in 1855 the title was abolished under the doctrine of lapse. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-prince">The Prince</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcot">Arcot</a> title survives.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/carnatic-sultanate.jpg" alt="Carnatic Sultanate" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Lemonjam · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Nawab.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1692 CE – 1855 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Arcot</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Saadatullah Khan, Chanda Sahib, Muhammad Ali Khan Wallajah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian, Tamil, Urdu</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nawabs of the Carnatic, Nawabs of Arcot, Carnatic State</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1774&amp;focus=carnatic-sultanate" aria-label="Carnatic Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6857 1697 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Carnatic Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7177 1953L7154 2063M7177 1953L7018 1385M7177 1953L7132 1789M7177 1953L7364 1601"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7154 2063h0M7018 1385h0M7132 1789h0M7364 1601h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7177 1953h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7177 1953h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Arcot. <a href="/atlas?year=1774&amp;focus=carnatic-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

